This is an anagram generator I wrote a little while back, basedon an article in Byte, Nov. 1987, by Mike Morton.I have included comments in the sources to indicate the originalparts as coded by Mr. Morton (if I missed any, please don't kill me,Mike!).Should any of you come up with a good user interface, preferablygraphical, then Mr. Morton would be interested in hearing fromyou. He can be reached at: ``mikem@uhccux.uhcc.hawaii.edu''(which eventually will be replaced by: ``mike@daba1.pegasus.com'').Added in this release are two filters, written by Sean Barret,``buzzard@eng.umd.edu''.  There is a pre- and a postfilter, one for sorting out and ``customizing'' the dictionary, and one for postprocessing, sorting  out unwanted anagrams. As he writes:|"...Still, I decided to make a go-round again with ag, an#d so wrote| these filters.  It is quite possible and even probable that some| good anagrams will get cut using 'postf'; still, like a good judicial| system, hopefully it'll be many more of the "bad" ones, percentage| wise."| .| .| .|"...it is the postfix word-length processing that cuts out| the vast majority of words."||"Both programs should be considered public domain.  I always| like getting credit for my ideas, but admittedly the programs| here are nothing astonishing.  They're not even bug-free."As you can see, he places the code in the public domain, free for anyone to use (even though it probably would be nice if you mentioned his name somewhere).This version fixes a bug which (so far) only occured on SPARC's,namely when dereferencing a NULL pointer in the ``parse_flags'' routine.  All the other compilers I've tried quite happily ignored the fact that this is illogical.I have also added the feature of reading the dictionary from stdin.If, when specifying input dictionary, you give it ``-d -'', ag willread the dictionary from stdin. Note, however, that only 1 phrasecan be processed when making use of this, as it isn't possible torewind standard input. Any additional phrases will be ignored by ag.This means that ag can be put in a pipeline, and the output fromany filter can be used as input.The genarator compiles with	make agand should run on any Unix; I've compiled and run it onHP-UX (6.5 - 7.0), Sun 3 (3.5, 4.0, 4.1.1), Sun4c (4.1.1), Sequent (DYNIX(R) V3.0.14 NFS  #18), Mac (Think C, MPW).The algorithm used to generate anagrams is very quick, an orderof magnitude faster than the program found on uunet.uu.net.(well, now this little thingy is also on uunet so...)As literally zillions of anagrams are generated, long phrasesshould be avoided, and the output redirected to a file (forlater humorous viewing..:-)Have Fun!Morten (morten@dcs.qmw.ac.uk)